The bound separates two regimes. If $S$ cannot span the $r$ factors, $\lambda_{\min}=0$ and $D_T$ falls only along the directions the run set covers, so a few well-chosen benchmarks help most. Once $S$ spans them, the bound falls as $1/(1+ck)$ whenever $\lambda_{\min}$ grows as fast as $ck$, which holds for a random run set when the loadings are spread out. With separated factors the transition is a knee near the number of factors; with a slowly decaying spectrum it is a gradual closure. Both regimes are tested on synthetic data in Section~\ref{sec:experiments}.

The needed run-set size follows from the excess over the floor in RMSE, which is the quantity of Section~\ref{sec:protocol}. With $F_T^{\mathrm{pred}}(S)$ as above, the model predicts
\begin{equation}
\text{excess}(k)=\sqrt{F_T^{\mathrm{pred}}(S_k)}-\sqrt{F_T^{\mathrm{pred}}(C)},
\end{equation}
and $k^*_{\mathrm{pred}}(\epsilon)$ is the smallest $k$ at which this is at most $\epsilon$. It uses the training spectrum and noise alone, not the measured floor, and we test it against the measured $k^*(\epsilon)$.
